  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Tuesday) \nIssued at 1143 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nScattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms will likely be \nongoing from Wednesday night into Thursday morning as the main \nupper low/trough axis move over the Plains. Heavy rain, which may\nlead to flooding and flash flooding, will continue to be a threat\nthrough at least the morning hours Thursday, perhaps lingering \nthrough the afternoon as the front passes through. A surface low \nwill scoot northeastward across northwest OK and southeast KS \nThursday morning, briefly lifting the surface boundary north as it\ndoes. As the surface low moves into eastern MO and southern IL, \nit will drag the trailing surface cold front into the forecast \narea again by mid Thursday afternoon. Additional convection is \nanticipated to develop along the frontal boundary as it pushes \nacross eastern OK and northwest AR. CAMs indicate that the severe \npotential with the passing frontal boundary stays relatively low, \nthough isolated strong thunderstorms cannot be ruled out in the \nvicinity of the front, given high values of CAPE still around. \nStill think the higher threat continues to be nuisance flooding, \nwith additional rainfall amounts from a quarter of an inch to half\nan inch, locally up to a couple of inches possible, associated with\nthe front itself. \n\nThe passing cold front on Thursday afternoon/evening will result \nin drier and much cooler conditions following it. Strong post-\nfrontal high pressure filtering down from Canada will bring a \nrefreshing, autumn airmass for Friday through the upcoming \nweekend. Expect mostly clear skies, with crisp overnight lows in \nthe upper 30s to 40s both for Friday night and Saturday night, and\npleasant daytime highs in the 60s to lower 70s through the \nweekend under surface high pressure. Temperatures begin to \nmoderate and trend warmer by early next week, trending back above \naverage by Tuesday. \n\nMejia",
